  "problem": "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 0,\\,0\\le z\\le 2+y,\\,1\\le x\\le y-z+3}.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that we have a three-dimensional type II region.\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={\u222d}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2+y}{\\int }_{1}^{y-z+3}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2+y}[x]_{x=1}^{x=y-z+3}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2+y}(y-z+3)-1\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2+y}y-z+2\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[(y+2)z-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=2+y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}((y+2)(2+y)-\\frac{(2+y)^{2}}{2})-0\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{y}^{2}+4y+4\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{2}+4y]}_{y=-2}^{y=0} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}+2(-2)^{2}+4(-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
